    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • The Volkswagen Group's Affordable BEV Quartet Has Almost Reached 150,000 Orders\n2026 Mazda MX-5 Roadster and RF Get Updated Inside and Outside; This Is How Much They Cost Now\n7 photos\nPhoto: Mazda\nThe Japanese automaker's local division in the Land Down Under wants to make Mazda MX-5's rivals green with envy when they see the latest updates for MY2026. But is the joke on them, since most other competitors have already switched to the 2027 model year?\nMazda is a small automaker, despite its continued large visibility triggered by iconic models like the MX-5 Miata roadster and the recent string of crossover SUV hits, which include the CX-50 produced in America as well as the regional CX-60, CX-70, CX-80, and CX-90 models. They’re even becoming known for their all-electric prowess, thanks to the Chinese-born Mazda 6e and CX-6e, recently.\nOver in Australia, though, the commotion isn’t common, pun intended: Mazda is bringing few and far-off novelties to the market, like the all-new Mazda CX-6e EV crossover SUV in July, the all-new CX-5 compact crossover SUV in March, or the Mazda M Day event in October 2026. Before the sale festival begins on October 17, 2026, though, Mazda has decided to go green with the 2026 MX-5 and update it thoroughly.\nThe Japanese automaker speaks of a “series of thoughtful enhancements inside and out for 2026, further elevating the experience on offer in the world’s best-selling two-seat sports car.” The iconic Mazda MX-5 Miata Roadster and RF get the newly introduced Zinc Green Metallic premium color, which has been introduced also in North America and on the Old Continent as part of the latest string of updates.\nIt debuts in Australia on the 2026 MX-5 Roadster and RF, taking “inspiration from the color and texture of zinc chromate primer – an undercoat used to enhance the durability of industrial products – and translates rational beauty into a sense of toughness expressed through color and texture.” The other hues remain unchanged: Snowflake White Pearl Mica, Soul Red Crystal Metallic, Deep Crystal Blue Mica, Jet Black Mica, Machine Grey Metallic and Aero Grey Metallic.\nThe 2026 Mazda MX-5 range, meanwhile, gets Autonomous Emergency Braking (AEB), which is “capable of detecting both pedestrians and cyclists (previously pedestrians only), providing greater confidence across a wider range of driving scenarios.” Lane-keep Assist System (LAS) is also added to the roster, while the MX-5 GT RS gets an exclusive additional perk.\nThe model introduces lightweight Recaro bucket seats trimmed in Nappa leather and genuine Alcantara with light grey stitching free of charge, as well as genuine Alcantara trim across the instrument panel, center console, console lid and door trim. “Green has been in high demand from our customers, and we’re excited to reintroduce it. This is the first time in 10 years that a green body colour has been available on a Mazda in Australia. It’s a fitting addition to the rest of the compelling updates for our globally revered sports car,” says Jarrod Gieschen, Mazda Australia Sales and Marketing Director.\nThe 2026 Mazda MX-5 price list kicks off with the G20 Roadster Manual version, starting at an MLP (Manufacturer’s List Price) of AU$44,590, which is the equivalent of approximately $31k at the current exchange rate. The G20 Roadster GT is available with a manual or automatic transmission for AU$51,240 or AU$52,740, while the G20 Roadster GT RS starts from AU$57,240. The prices for the RF vary between AU$55,740 and AU$61,740, meanwhile.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\n2026 Mazda MX-5 Roadster 2026 Mazda MX-5 RF Mazda MX-5 Mazda MX-5 2026MY pricing Australia\nRead Press Release\nAbout the author: Aurel Niculescu\nAurel has aimed high all his life (literally, at 16 he was flying gliders all by himself) so in 2006 he switched careers and got hired as a writer at his favorite magazine. Since then, his work has been published both by print and online outlets, most recently right here, on autoevolution. Full profile\nUP NEXT Google Releases a Google Maps and Waze Fuel Price Feature All Users Need",
